 p=0;for(let k=0;k<L.length;k++){if((k<=li&&H.test(L[k]))||(k<li&&G.test(L[k])))s=p;if(k>li&&(H.test(L[k])||G.test(L[k]))){e=p;break;}p+=L[k].length+1;}
 s=Math.max(s,i-1500);e=Math.min(e,i+m[0].length+300);return T.slice(s,e).trim();};
const PROMPT="You are the closing enforcer. A debater's closing round may not contradict what the same debater stated in its earlier rounds.\nRead each side's closing round against that side's own earlier rounds. The closing rounds were already read once before the judges sat. Answer every candidate listed below; each was named by a judge after it read the debate. Give each candidate its link.\nA contradiction is two statements that cannot both be true. Two sentences of the same closing round that cannot both be true are also a contradiction: quote the first as EARLIER [<round 4>] and the second as CLOSING [<round 4>]. A closing sentence that asserts again, as the debater's own, what it withdrew, dropped or gave up in an earlier round IS a contradiction.\nIt is NOT a contradiction when the closing sentence: grants, concedes, narrows or withdraws something; states or quotes the other side's position; answers a press with a further reason the earlier sentence leaves room for; or differs only in wording.\nRead a withdrawal by its meaning, not its words: a closing sentence that says again in other words what the earlier sentence withdrew, dropped or gave up, or rests its reason on it, asserts it again. \"Differs only in wording\" clears two sentences that make the same claim; it never clears a closing sentence that makes again a claim the debater withdrew. A closing sentence that states only what the debater kept, or rests the same conclusion on other rows, does not assert it again. When the earlier sentence rests no weight on a verse or row, a closing sentence that uses that verse or row as a footing for the same link or the same purpose asserts it again.\nRead each candidate's closing sentence together with the rows of its CLOSING BLOCK: a closing sentence that draws its point from a row (\"So\", \"Therefore\", \"Thus\") asserts what that row says, so if the earlier sentence withdrew, dropped or rested nothing on that row, the close asserts it again.\nFor each contradiction, quote both sentences word for word, each from the round it stands in, and name the link it belongs to: the link whose block in the closing round holds the closing sentence, or, where the block names no link, the link the earlier sentence supports. The defender's links are the numbers of its DECLARED CHAIN in [CR1]; the challenger's links are the numbers of its LINK blocks in [DR3].\nReply with one line per contradiction and per candidate, and nothing else:\nCLOSE-CONTRADICTION: <candidate id or NEW> -- <DEFENDER|CHALLENGER> -- LINK <n> -- CONFIRMED | NOT A CONTRADICTION -- EARLIER [<round>] \"<words>\" -- CLOSING [<round 4>] \"<words>\" -- <one sentence: why both cannot be true, or why they can>\nIf you find none and there is no candidate, reply: CLOSE-CONTRADICTION: NONE";  
